There is a company somewhere, involved in commercial fantasy-based
games (For some reason the initials M.E.R.P. spring to mind, although
I've also heard of initials I.C.E. - can't remember what either stands
for, although the first is almost certainly Middle-Earth
Role-Playing): that has invented names for all of the Nazguls. I've
seen these names on a LotR collectible card game several years ago.
They even made up a *history* for each of them, which at the least
does not actively conflict with anything Tolkien has written.

The other famous roguelike game, Angband, has "borrowed" all the
Nazgul names from this. So, as a result, we get names for the other
seven:

Uvatha the Horseman
Adunaphel the Quiet
Akhorahil the Blind
Ren the Unclean
Ji Indur Dawndeath
Dwar, Lord of Waw
Hoarmurath of Dir

plus Tolkien's own canonical "Khamul, the Black Easterling". And "The
Witch-King of Angmar", who does not have so much a name as a job
description.

(Which implies that the "Gothmog, the lieutenant of Morgul", mentioned
in LotR as throwing Sauron's reinforcements into the Battle of the
Pelennor after the destruction of the Witch-King - and presumably
named after the High Captain of Balrogs in "The Silmarillion" - may
not have been a Nazgul, but a human servant of Morgoth or possibly
even a leading Orcish general. This would be supported by the fact
that the winds from the south had cleared away the fumes of Orodruin
and the sun could now shine on the battlefield, so the remaining
Nazguls would have been considerably weakened and their own influence
on the battle lessened: thus the lieutenant is more likely to have
been one who could stand the sunlight, and therefore human but named
after the demon-lord of old.)

